🏠 Can you buy property in Dubai and have the rental income cover your mortgage?

Yes—and that’s exactly why many investors today use a mortgage not only to buy a home, but also as a tool to build an income-generating asset.

Currently, mortgage rates in Dubai start at around 4% per year, while potential rental yields, depending on the project and location, can reach 7–11% annually.

Here’s how it works in practice:

Let’s say you purchase an apartment with a monthly mortgage payment of AED 4,500–7,000. If the property is located in a high-demand area with strong rental potential, the rental income may partially—or even fully—cover your monthly mortgage payments.

However, there’s one important point.

This strategy doesn’t work with every property. Choosing the wrong area or project can significantly reduce your returns and extend your investment’s payback period.

That’s why, before buying, it’s important to evaluate:
🏠the area’s long-term growth potential;
🏠actual rental demand;
🏠the property’s liquidity;
🏠projected return on investment;
🏠the size of the down payment and your monthly mortgage payments.

Choosing the right property is what transforms a mortgage from a financial obligation into an effective investment tool.

